**Lemma 4.1 (global inequality with precisely four zeros).** On the
entire compact ellipse $\alpha u^2+\beta v^2=1$, set
$k(u,v)=[-f(u,v)]_+$ and $H_p(u,v)=|u|+p k(u,v)$. For all
sufficiently small positive $p$, the selected polynomial satisfies

$$
P_{a(p)}(u,v)\geq H_p(u,v),
\tag{EP.23}
$$

with equality exactly at the four contacts of Lemma 3.2.

Proof. At zero,
$P_{a(0)}-|u|=(|u|-1)^2/2$, whose only ellipse zeros are the four
corners. Choose disjoint small corner arcs on which $u,v$ and $f$ have
their strict corner signs and $d=|u|-1$ is an analytic coordinate.
Outside those arcs compactness gives a positive minimum of the base
difference. Uniform convergence of $P_{a(p)}$ and boundedness of $k$
preserve strict positivity there for small $p$.

Inside each arc the difference is the analytic function
$a(p)\cdot m_j(d)-(1+d+p k_j(d))$. Its value and first derivative
vanish at $d_j(p)$. Its second $d$ derivative equals one at $p=0$
throughout the arc, and stays at least $1/2$ after shrinking the arcs
and parameter interval. Taylor's integral formula bounds it below by
$(d-d_j(p))^2/4$. This proves the inequality and its complete zero set.
Axes, zeros of $f$ and all remote ellipse points are included in the
compact complement argument. $\square$

